bound-lock/1 about="# Attributed empirical declarations and missing evidence\n\nRecord only what the supplied scope attributes to the sandbox. No dataset was opened or analyzed in this work. Missing report, protocol and artifact bytes remain named obligations; a declaration or digest does not itself audit a result.\n\nDeclared conditions:\n- Attribution is explicit; independent audit status remains unreviewed\n- Phase statuses are reported claims, not automatically verified facts\n- No result is promoted from an identifier or a page metadata prefix\n- No experiment, simulation, download or p-value reconstruction\n\nBoundary: The separate sandbox report was referenced but not supplied. The claimed lack of exposure and preregistration chronology are attribution, not independently checked history.\n\nEvidence status: declared by sandbox; not audited. T2 remains pending approval.\n" at=witness:declared-relations by=target form=alphabet measure=text role=writes scope=prose/en/contracts/research-evidence-register shape=docs/contracts/research-evidence-register.md value=lock
bound-lock/1 about="# Claim, assumptions, evidence and consumer coverage\n\nEvery entry identifies its proposition, parameter domain, evidence class, consumed contract and unresolved obligations. A finite case is not a universal proof; a source identity is not correctness. T2 and missing exact claims stay visibly pending.\n\nDeclared conditions:\n- Existing proof identities are retained\n- No renumbering or implicit domain expansion\n- Unproved regions remain named\n- Release selection and delivery are separate from theory acceptance\n\nBoundary: The current 0.4.6 selection and its closed Root do not certify the complete 0.4.7 scope or authorize publication.\n\nEvidence status: coverage registry with explicit open regions. T2 remains pending approval.\n" at=witness:declared-relations by=target form=alphabet measure=text role=writes scope=prose/en/contracts/theory-coverage shape=docs/contracts/theory-coverage.md value=lock
bound-lock/1 about="# Contributing\n\nClone, `npm ci && npm run build`. Bring a counterexample with its inputs and printed output.\n\nOpen research demands are named in `TARGET.bound`; a result needs its model, conditions and falsifier. No count is inferred from missing observations.\n\nLines land through the instrument. Submit proposed lines for review rather than editing an admitted lock or its rendered pages.\n" at=witness:041/retain-declared-documentation by=target form=alphabet measure=text role=writes scope=prose/en/guide shape=CONTRIBUTING.md value=lock
bound-lock/1 about="# Declared finite linear influence identities\n\nFor the declared finite synchronous family, complete mixing has drift -Cov_n(alpha,x); constant alpha preserves the mean. Homogeneous transport has drift a Cov_n(W^T 1,x), not generally a covariance with degree. Correlation size requires an explicit covariance law and a nonzero denominator.\n\nWith a declared nonnegative normalized left-stationary vector of W, homogeneous alpha also preserves the weighted mean.\n\nDeclared conditions:\n- n>=1; rational finite vectors in executable cases\n- Row-stochastic W; alpha in [0,1]^n; zero noise; linear coordinates\n- The homogeneous transport identity additionally requires constant alpha\n- K_corr uses the declared covariance, not keys, Kish, accuracy or causal independence\n\nBoundary: T2 is excluded. Finite identities are kernel-checked over rationals; no general real convergence or stochastic covariance theorem is claimed by the finite cases.\n\nEvidence status: Lean identities plus exact finite vectors; covariance evolution derivation separated from its scalar normalization. T2 remains pending approval.\n" at=witness:declared-relations by=target form=alphabet measure=text role=writes scope=prose/en/contracts/linear-influence shape=docs/contracts/linear-influence.md value=lock
bound-lock/1 about="# Decoder-specific copy thresholds and endpoint scope\n\nFor p in (1/2,1), positive gap g=(1-c)(2p-1)-c is equivalent to c<1-1/(2p), and this threshold lies strictly between zero and one half. At c=1, equal positive replication per lineage and a declared joint product Bernoulli law reduce fair majority to the finite K-vote binomial formula, including half the tie mass.\n\nDeclared conditions:\n- Decoder and tie rule are named\n- Product law is declared and checked for each finite PMF; pairwise marginals do not substitute\n- c=1 equal-vote conclusion requires equal lineage replication; unequal weights use their actual weighted decoder\n- Existing 0.4.6 off-diagonal and conditional diagonal contracts retain all their original assumptions\n\nBoundary: The positive-gap algebra is not a kernel proof of a Hoeffding inequality. Uniform concentration needs the joint-law hypotheses and its concentration proof; the complete uniform bound in Lean stays open. Generic optimal blind-decoder limits and monotonicity of the decoder gap remain open.\n\nEvidence status: Lean algebraic boundary, finite endpoint PMFs and explicit inventory; no replacement of published probability theorems. T2 remains pending approval.\n" at=witness:declared-relations by=target form=alphabet measure=text role=writes scope=prose/en/contracts/copy-threshold-boundaries shape=docs/contracts/copy-threshold-boundaries.md value=lock
bound-lock/1 about="# Estimates and decisions have distinct contracts\n\nA finite decision is evaluated against a declared action space, state law, loss and tie rule. The same estimate can select different actions under different loss tables. An estimate or K_corr alone does not specify an action, accuracy or a universal scale of ceilings and floors.\n\nDeclared conditions:\n- Finite named states and actions; normalized rational prior\n- Every action-state loss is declared in a fixed loss unit\n- Return every minimizing action; do not break an undeclared tie\n- Estimation units and decision loss units remain distinct\n\nBoundary: These are conditional finite decision tables, not a universal product law. The estimation-to-decision bridge remains open outside a declared operation.\n\nEvidence status: definition and exact countermodels; no new runtime estimator or decision authority. T2 remains pending approval.\n" at=witness:declared-relations by=target form=alphabet measure=text role=writes scope=prose/en/contracts/decision-estimation shape=docs/contracts/decision-estimation.md value=lock
bound-lock/1 about="# Experiment 1a: protocol, no measurement\n\nThe versioned model-collapse-1a protocol is prepared independently of dataset selection. No owner-selected dataset, license, bound training/aggregation model or finite analysis design has been supplied; no data were retrieved and no empirical experiment was run. The result is unmeasured, not zero, pass, fail or a law.\n\nFreeze the protocol digest before retrieval. A separate run manifest must bind the owner-selected snapshot/license, model and training provenance, lineage mechanism, volume grid, truth/quality endpoint, sampling units/dependence blocks, exploratory/holdout split, seed, stopping rule, finite hypothesis, effect threshold and valid uncertainty. Freeze its digest before retrieval, and freeze the final exploratory analysis before opening holdout. Digests identify bytes; they do not certify a signed admission or delivery.\n\nRecord prior exposure, including this session; unknown training exposure remains unknown. Keep related units and duplicates from leaking across splits. Reuse O5 uncertainty only under its actual sampling/calibration hypotheses. Preserve failed runs and report every prespecified outcome whether the falsifier succeeds or fails. No posthoc endpoint or optional stopping is authorized.\n\nA finite within-lineage curve that continues falling is a candidate empirical challenge only to a specified finite prediction or justified bound. It does not by itself refute a positive asymptotic floor. The training process, quality and lineage assumptions must be supplied; no model is invented from the experiment title. Causal interpretations, including the unspecified historical (ii)/(iii), remain unmeasured without an observable intervention, justified assignment and a defined estimand. Descriptive observations cannot close causal identification.\n\nConditional lineage identification, causal subgroup recovery and oracle-free reference remain open. This protocol changes no algebra, M/3-M/4, wire, signed base or protocol instrument.\n" at=witness:obligations046/O9 by=target form=alphabet measure=text role=writes scope=prose/en/contracts/model-collapse-1a shape=docs/contracts/model-collapse-1a.md value=lock
bound-lock/1 about="# Extension, named lineage and effective participation\n\nExtension is the supplied set of coordinate/interval pairs in A5, compared at fixed coordinate interpretation, resolution and context. Equal extensions identify one extensional class under that convention. They do not identify historical actors, establish a shared cause or prove probabilistic independence. A named lineage is a declared model grouping with a supplied generative/ancestry mechanism, membership and weights. Its name is not observational evidence.\n\nJoint independence is factorization of the declared joint probability law for all joint events. A conditional statement names its conditioning variable and requires factorization in every positive-mass stratum. Different names, disjoint extensions and pairwise zero covariance do not suffice. The fair-bit XOR triple has independent pairs and fails joint factorization.\n\nFor finite nonnegative masses with positive total, set w_i=a_i/sum(a) and K_eff=1/sum(w_i^2). It is dimensionless weight participation, between1 and the positive-support size; equal positive weights give that size. It is not a count of causally independent extensions. For a symmetric PSD covariance C with positive trace, normalized nonnegative eigenvalues instead give spectral participation trace(C)^2/tr(C^2). These are different inputs and need a model before they can be equated. Existing participationRatio checks symmetry and nonnegative diagonal but does not certify PSD. A true covariance constructed from the declared error rows supplies PSD; passing arbitrary matrices directly does not. Zero variation is unmeasured. In O4, K_eff=K and identification from population moments apply only in the declared uniform known-p family; nonuniform moment collisions remain.\n\nA7 concerns fixed coordinates, resolution, context and ceilings under added live narrowing constraints. Withdrawal and witnessed local widening may reopen freedom on the same coordinate. A sum of coordinate log-cardinal freedoms is not automatically joint Shannon entropy. T90 equates log-cardinality with entropy for nonempty finite uniformly distributed admissible states at fixed resolution; it does not establish the identity for arbitrary priors or a physical phase interpretation.\n\nA9 is a representation convention, not a convergence theorem. T99 concerns finite records and worlds, an acyclic reachable dependency graph and total supplied lattice operations: evaluation takes finitely many operations. Reachable ancestral cycles reject, while unreachable cycles do not affect a query. An arbitrary monotone iteration still needs its carrier, operator, seed and progress/height hypotheses; a monotone reflection on incomparable singleton intervals has a two-cycle. Different hypotheses and missing proofs are not a fork.\n\nThe historical C1 independence-is-distance wording supplies no probabilistic factorization. Definitions settle meaning only. Conditional lineage identification, causal subgroup recovery and oracle-free reference remain open. This companion text changes no signed base, algebra, M/3-M/4 or wire." at=witness:obligations046/definitions by=target form=interval measure=count needs=vectors/laws/definitions.native.json|contracts/extension-lineage-participation.json|docs/contracts/extension-lineage-participation.md|proofs/definitions-046/declaration.json|vectors/laws/policy.json|src/laws/laws.bound|vectors/laws/a7-t90.json|vectors/laws/a9-t99.json|samples/laws/definitions/yes/sample.json|samples/laws/definitions/no/sample.json role=demands scope=publication/evidence/definitions value=0..0
bound-lock/1 about="# Extension, named lineage and effective participation\n\nExtension is the supplied set of coordinate/interval pairs in A5, compared at fixed coordinate interpretation, resolution and context. Equal extensions identify one extensional class under that convention. They do not identify historical actors, establish a shared cause or prove probabilistic independence. A named lineage is a declared model grouping with a supplied generative/ancestry mechanism, membership and weights. Its name is not observational evidence.\n\nJoint independence is factorization of the declared joint probability law for all joint events. A conditional statement names its conditioning variable and requires factorization in every positive-mass stratum. Different names, disjoint extensions and pairwise zero covariance do not suffice. The fair-bit XOR triple has independent pairs and fails joint factorization.\n\nFor finite nonnegative masses with positive total, set w_i=a_i/sum(a) and K_eff=1/sum(w_i^2). It is dimensionless weight participation, between1 and the positive-support size; equal positive weights give that size. It is not a count of causally independent extensions. For a symmetric PSD covariance C with positive trace, normalized nonnegative eigenvalues instead give spectral participation trace(C)^2/tr(C^2). These are different inputs and need a model before they can be equated. Existing participationRatio checks symmetry and nonnegative diagonal but does not certify PSD. A true covariance constructed from the declared error rows supplies PSD; passing arbitrary matrices directly does not. Zero variation is unmeasured. In O4, K_eff=K and identification from population moments apply only in the declared uniform known-p family; nonuniform moment collisions remain.\n\nA7 concerns fixed coordinates, resolution, context and ceilings under added live narrowing constraints. Withdrawal and witnessed local widening may reopen freedom on the same coordinate. A sum of coordinate log-cardinal freedoms is not automatically joint Shannon entropy. T90 equates log-cardinality with entropy for nonempty finite uniformly distributed admissible states at fixed resolution; it does not establish the identity for arbitrary priors or a physical phase interpretation.\n\nA9 is a representation convention, not a convergence theorem. T99 concerns finite records and worlds, an acyclic reachable dependency graph and total supplied lattice operations: evaluation takes finitely many operations. Reachable ancestral cycles reject, while unreachable cycles do not affect a query. An arbitrary monotone iteration still needs its carrier, operator, seed and progress/height hypotheses; a monotone reflection on incomparable singleton intervals has a two-cycle. Different hypotheses and missing proofs are not a fork.\n\nThe historical C1 independence-is-distance wording supplies no probabilistic factorization. Definitions settle meaning only. Conditional lineage identification, causal subgroup recovery and oracle-free reference remain open. This companion text changes no signed base, algebra, M/3-M/4 or wire.\n" at=witness:obligations046/O8 by=target form=alphabet measure=text role=writes scope=prose/en/contracts/extension-lineage-participation shape=docs/contracts/extension-lineage-participation.md value=lock
bound-lock/1 about="# Finite measures on declared regions\n\nLet U be a finite set of named atoms and let w:U -> Q be nonnegative. Define m(A)=sum_{a in A} w(a) for A subset U. Atom names identify members of this declared support; they do not establish authentication or physical completeness.\n\nFor every atom, 1_A(a)+1_B(a)=1_(A union B)(a)+1_(A intersection B)(a). Multiplying by w(a) and summing gives m(A)+m(B)=m(A union B)+m(A intersection B). In particular, disjoint supports imply additive measurements. Overlap may still have zero weight; equality of totals does not imply disjoint supports.\n\nFor a finite family A_i, put r(a)=the number of distinct regions containing a. Then sum_i m(A_i)-m(union_i A_i)=sum_{a:r(a)>0}(r(a)-1)w(a). This exact nonnegative excess explains double counting. Adding a duplicate delivery of the same atom does not create a second support element. Two different weights under the same atom identity refuse as conflicting inputs.\n\nNo theorem here infers a lattice's distributivity, a cancellation operation, an exact withdrawal, a tokenizer law, a clock law or an authorization. Those require their respective contracts. Selector names are not supports; a host must supply the actual identified members. An unassigned atom and an empty declared region remain visible.\n\nThe pairwise union/intersection identity, disjoint additivity and the nonnegative union bound are kernel-checked in `lean/Obligations/RegionMeasure.lean`. The arbitrary finite-family excess formula above remains a written finite-sum derivation. The TypeScript implementation's correspondence to Lean is tested on bounded vectors, not kernel-proved. These three evidence scopes remain separate.\n\n## Public consumption\n\n`finiteRegionMeasure` and type `RegionMeasure` are available through the candidate `@lapxo/obligations/measures` export. The existing subpath remains unchanged. Measurements require an explicit model, unit, finite support and deterministic input-row budget. Topos supplies selector interpretation; Bound applies authority and ceilings.\n\nThe supplemental reader seals declared inputs and replays finite mathematical cases. Compilation evidence is reused only for the same proof sources and pinned toolchain; it does not claim a new Lean execution during receipt replay. Owner admission and release delivery are separate acts. T2 remains outside this selection.\n" at=witness:declared-measures-and-provenance by=target form=alphabet measure=text role=writes scope=prose/en/contracts/region-measure shape=docs/contracts/region-measure.md value=lock
bound-lock/1 about="# In phase\n\nA claim carries a size and a direction, how much it narrows and the turn it landed on, and a withdrawal is the same size the other way: two hundred claims in phase make the square of their number and the same two hundred strewn make its order, while a write and its withdrawal at one epoch leave nothing and at different epochs do not cancel.\n\n## Run it\n\n```ts\nimport { coherence, interfere } from '@lapxo/obligations/views/field';\n\nconst span = { lo: 0, hi: 0 };\nlet held = 11;\nconst next = (): number => { held = (held * 1103515245 + 12345) % 2147483648; return held / 2147483648; };\nconst together = Array.from({ length: 32 }, (_, i) => ({ origin: `o${i}`, epoch: 8, span }));\nconst strewn = Array.from({ length: 32 }, (_, i) => ({ origin: `o${i}`, epoch: Math.floor(next() * 8), span }));\n\nconsole.log('in phase  ', interfere(together, 8), 'coherence', coherence(together, 8).toFixed(2));\nconsole.log('strewn    ', interfere(strewn, 8).toFixed(0), 'coherence', coherence(strewn, 8).toFixed(2));\nconsole.log('their number is 32, and its square is', 32 ** 2);\nconsole.log('why       claims that land at one turn of the period add as the square of their number, and the same claims strewn across it add as its order');\n```\n\n```\nin phase   1024 coherence 1.00\nstrewn     50 coherence 0.22\ntheir number is 32, and its square is 1024\nwhy       claims that land at one turn of the period add as the square of their number, and the same claims strewn across it add as its order\n```\n\nIts origins meet.\n\n## What it rests on\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n  c0[\"coherence\"]\n  c1[\"interfere\"]\n  l0([\"amplitude has sign and phase\"])\n  c0 --> l0\n  c1 --> l0\n  classDef shown fill:#2da44e,color:#ffffff,stroke:#2da44e\n  class l0 shown\n```\n\n## Source\n\n[Its source](../../examples/release/in-phase.ts) is one of the examples of obligations, and it runs as it is written, against the built package, with nothing compiled for it.\n" at=witness:041/retain-declared-documentation by=target form=alphabet measure=text role=writes scope=prose/en/learn-in-phase shape=docs/learn/in-phase.md value=lock
bound-lock/1 about="# Key identifiers do not identify an independent joint law\n\nFix two key identifiers and the same declared provenance metadata. Model C samples one fair bit B and sets X=Y=B. Model I samples independent fair bits X and Y. The key identifiers and provenance declarations are unchanged across the models.\n\nIn C, P(X=0,Y=0)=1/2 while P(X=0)P(Y=0)=1/4. The factorization claim is false. In I, every joint atom has mass 1/4 and the displayed binary joint law factors. Both laws are compatible with the same identifiers and labels, so those metadata do not entail independence. This countermodel refutes that implication without a statistical experiment.\n\nUnder a declared common-control model, two keys may share one named control group. Splitting their provenance labels changes the declared grouping, not the response law. The vector reports group counts only as declarations and never as identified independent K.\n\nThe existing marginalInformation computes one bit of information between identical fair bits and zero between independent fair bits under these exact priors. Its numeric policy remains explicit. Renaming a lineage changes attribution but does not change this calculation. These cases reuse extension-lineage-participation@1; they add no causal estimator and perform no signature generation or authority admission.\n\n`lean/Obligations/KeyLineage.lean` checks nonnegativity, normalization, equal fair marginals, factorization of the independent table and failure of factorization of the copied table. It also checks that either table can be paired with the same arbitrary metadata. The numerical information outputs reuse the existing API and are executable vectors, not a new Lean entropy theorem.\n\n## Public consumption\n\nThis contract reuses the published `marginalInformation` API and `extension-lineage-participation@1`. It adds no K estimator, key verifier or admission engine. Distinct keys and declared groups remain attribution; a supplied factorized probability law is a separate premise.\n\nThe supplemental reader seals declared inputs and replays finite mathematical cases. Compilation evidence is reused only for the same proof sources and pinned toolchain; it does not claim a new Lean execution during receipt replay. Owner admission and release delivery are separate acts. T2 remains outside this selection.\n" at=witness:declared-measures-and-provenance by=target form=alphabet measure=text role=writes scope=prose/en/contracts/key-lineage-independence shape=docs/contracts/key-lineage-independence.md value=lock
bound-lock/1 about="# Measures beside the states\n\nThe pure API @lapxo/obligations/measures does not change FREE, REQUIRED, FORBIDDEN or CONFLICT. Observed association never establishes independence.\n\n## Descriptive contract\nerrorMatrix takes explicit binary event/origin/error rows and minCells >= 2. Identical redelivery is idempotent; conflicting observations refuse; incomplete shared events are unmeasured. covariance is population covariance of the handed rows, divided by their event count. participationRatio describes effective rank; zero variation is unmeasured.\n\nspectrum requires absolute, relative and maxRotations. Asymmetric or materially non-PSD matrices refuse. commonMass is the largest observed variance fraction, not a probability of false agreement. loadings require an isolated top eigenvalue; repeated eigenspaces have no unique top loading. commonMassOf requires an explicit cutoff and whole eigenspaces. The unanimity no-sample prints observed commonMass >= 0.5 beside FREE, without claiming strength.\n\ndescentCount counts distinct proper descendants in a declared restsOn graph. Identical edges add nothing. Missing declarations remain unmeasured; duplicate node declarations and cycles refuse. Declared descent is not observed activity or independence.\n\n## Controlled procedures\nunanimityFloor and unanimityThreshold implement the declared T1 special case. With P_k=(1-c)p^k(n-1)^(1-k), F_k=(c+P_k)/(c+P_k+(1-c)(1-p)^k). For nondegenerate parameters, F_(k+1) >= F_k iff p*c >= (1-p-p/(n-1))*P_k. The c=.01,p=.1,n=2 no-sample decreases before k=3; its threshold is 3. dissentExperiment and interestExperiment report conditional counts under explicitly handed generators: xorshift32, nonzero seed and trial count. Their denominators are events and falseEvents, and zero events are unmeasured. Interest is an explicitly handed bias toward one wrong value, not inferred from a missing observation. E3: a school answers truth with probability q, otherwise its own wrong value. These are controlled procedures, never laws or state changes.\n\n## Native publication evidence\nobservations, spectrum, unanimity and descent each have yes/no cases in samples/measures/<group>.json and installed-instrument captures in vectors/measures/<group>.cli.json. M/3 has samples/measures/m3.json and vectors/measures/m3.cli.json. Each vector ran on two independent places, repeated the positive reading byte-for-byte and refused its negative by name. State-preservation tests compare all four actual algebraic states before and after measurements.\n\n## Model boundary\nM/3 below is the only reference model. The older descriptive D/commonMass and independence claims are superseded. modelRequired returns unmeasured for an absent model; no measurement silently supplies a causal model. Factor attribution, dissent and probe results are conditional on M/3's handed parameters, not empirical independence certificates.\n\n# obligations 0.4.0 — model M/3 (authoritative contract)\n\nM/3 is the reference conditional model shipped beside the descriptive measures. Every M/3\nresult carries `model=M/3`, its handed parameters and its conditions. The descriptive\nmeasures are independent of M/3. Nothing changes a state. This document supersedes all\nearlier M/1, M/2 and amendment texts.\n\n## 1. Definition\nWitnesses W (unique identifiers); cells with truth t and integer cardinality n ≥ 2;\nfactors F, each with unique identifier, sharing set a_f ⊆ W, firing probability c_f and a\nunique declared index (total order); private error probability p_w for every witness\ninvolved. Per cell, independently:\n1. each factor fires independently with probability c_f;\n2. a witness is governed by the fired factor containing it with the lowest index, if\n   any; otherwise its private error fires with probability p_w;\n3. labels: each fired governing factor is a factor label; each privately erring witness\n   is a private label (namespaces are distinct: a factor label and a private label can\n   never merge even with identical identifier strings); every label draws one wrong\n   value uniformly from the n−1 wrong values; draws of distinct labels are mutually\n   independent and independent of all firing and private indicators; everyone else says t.\nSingleton factors (|a_f| = 1) are retained in declared order; the generator is never\nnormalized.\n\n## 2. Validation (refuse by name)\nS ⊆ W and S nonempty; every a_f ⊆ W; unique witness and factor identifiers; unique factor\nindices; c_f, p_w ∈ [0,1]; n integer ≥ 2; an explicitly handed p_w for every witness in\nS and in every sharing set touching S; a sharing set with duplicate members is refused.\n\n## 3. Exact evaluator\nEnumerate fired subsets of the factors touching S (2^|touching|) and private patterns of\nungoverned members of S (2^|free|). In each configuration, erring witnesses are grouped by\nlabel; L = number of labels; weights = witnesses per label. Collisions of wrong values\nfollow set partitions π of the labels with\nP(π) = (n−1)^{↓|π|} / (n−1)^L (falling factorial), and P(π) = 0 when |π| > n−1.\nEach partition yields block totals; unanimity ⇔ no correct witness and one block;\nmajority = strictly more than |S|/2 witnesses on one value; ties and pluralities ≤ half\nare \"no majority\".\nExactness: all c_f, p_w are handed as rationals (or decimal strings converted exactly);\naccumulation is in rational arithmetic; displayed decimals are rounded half-even to a\ndeclared number of digits. Budget: before any work, compute\nwork = Σ_configurations Bell(L) ≤ `evaluator/budget` (declared); if exceeded, return\n`unmeasured` with cause `evaluator/budget` and no partial accumulation.\nOutputs per (S, x): P_true, P_false (false unanimity), P_fired (false unanimity with some\ntouching factor fired), P_dissent = 1 − P_true − P_false (identity, asserted),\nmaj_true∧dissent, maj_false∧dissent, maj_none∧dissent (exclusive, sum = P_dissent).\nClosed form admitted only for P_true = Π_{f touches S}(1−c_f) · Π_{w∈S}(1−p_w).\n\n## 4. Quantities and denominators\n```\nΦ   = P_false / (P_true + P_false)        false-unanimity share\nD   = P_fired / (P_true + P_false)        factor-attribution share\nreliability = 1 − Φ, labelled \"P(correct | unanimous) under M/3 with handed parameters\"\nP(false maj | dissent)            = maj_false∧dissent / P_dissent\nP(false maj | any maj ∧ dissent)  = maj_false∧dissent / (maj_true∧dissent + maj_false∧dissent)\n```\nA ratio with zero denominator is `unmeasured` with cause; a zero numerator over a positive\ndenominator is a measured 0. Φ − D is private coincidence; 1 − D is never reliability.\n\n## 5. Dissent\nTheorem: if a factor covering S fires and no fired factor of lower index touches S\npartially, S is unanimous; disagreement inside S certifies this configuration did not\noccur — nothing more. False majorities under dissent remain possible through partial\nfactors and are reported through the two conditionals above. T1 and T5 stay controlled\nexperiments with declared PRNG, seed, trials and denominators (T5 uses the second\nconditional).\n\n## 6. Probe update\nFor a witness u private-only and independent of every factor touching S (or, with its\ntotal marginal error probability, labelled so):\n```\na = Φ · p_u/(n−1);  b = (1−Φ)·(1−p_u);  Φ′ = a/(a+b);  unmeasured iff a+b = 0\n```\nStrict improvement Φ′ < Φ holds for 0 < Φ < 1 iff p_u < (n−1)/n; boundary cases are\nvalid (Φ=0, p_u=0.2 → 0; Φ=1, p_u=0.2 → 1; p_u=1 → 1).\n\n## 7. Identification and fitting\nLet, for each witness w,\n```\nr_w = (1−p_w) · Π_{singleton f ∋ w} (1−c_f)        unresolved singleton/private survival\ns_w = r_w · Π_{shared f ∋ w} (1−c_f)                total correctness probability\ns_uv = P(e_u = 0 ∧ e_v = 0)\n```\nPopulation identification (declared sharing sets, s_u > 0 for every involved witness):\nlog(s_uv/(s_u·s_v)) = Σ_{shared f ∋ u,v} −log(1−c_f) is linear in −log(1−c_f); if the\npair × shared-factor system has full column rank, every shared c_f is identified, and then\nr_w from s_w; r_w's decomposition into p_w and singleton c_f is unresolved by error data.\nStructure is never discovered by a measure. Finite-data fitting is a separate declared\nprocedure: constrained least squares on the log system, declared budget, residual\ndiagnostics against a declared tolerance, bootstrap uncertainty with declared B/seed/PRNG,\npooling only under declared parameter compatibility; no pseudocounts, no clamping, no\npromotion of fitted structure; zero joint-correct counts or incompatible moments →\n`model/M misfit`, no c_f reported. Φ and D are reported only with handed parameters and\nlabelled `handed`, never `fitted`.\n\n## 8. Time, stake, commonMass\nFreshness and interest enter only as hypothetical factors with declared firing\nprobabilities; otherwise annotations and `unmeasured`. Descriptive commonMass is read as\nexcess over 1/|S| only as deviation from the equal-variance independent reference; it\nnever identifies dependence.\n\n## 9. Captured vectors (exact; rational reference; decimals rounded half-even, 8 digits)\n- two witnesses, covering c=1/20, p=1/5, n=64: P_true 0.608, P_false 0.05060317,\n  P_fired 0.05, P_dissent 0.34139683, Φ 0.07683409, D 0.07591825.\n- impossible conditioning (no factors, p_A=0, p_B=1): P_true = P_false = 0, P_dissent 1,\n  Φ, D unmeasured (cause: no unanimity mass).\n- three witnesses, covering {A,B,C} c=1/5 idx 0, partial {A,B} c=3/10 idx 1, p=0:\n  P_true 0.56, P_false 0.2, P_dissent 0.24, maj_false∧dissent 0.24, Φ = D = 0.26315789,\n  P(false maj | dissent) = 1, P(false maj | any maj ∧ dissent) = 1.\n- earlier singleton {A} c=3/10 idx 0, covering {A,B} c=1/5 idx 1, p=1/5: Φ 0.28387293,\n  D 0.28316249, P_dissent 0.49953016; merged-singleton no-sample Φ 0.35944805.\n- four witnesses, factors {0,1} and {2,3} each c=1/2, p=0: P_dissent 47/63 = 0.74603175,\n  maj_none∧dissent 47/63, P(false maj | dissent) = 0 (measured), P(false maj | any maj ∧\n  dissent) unmeasured (no majority mass), Φ = D = 1/64.\n- probe (Φ 0.07683409, n=64): p_u=0.2 → 0.00033; 0.98 → 0.06080; 0.99 → 0.11566; 1 → 1;\n  Φ=0,p_u=0.2 → 0; Φ=1,p_u=0.2 → 1.\n- identification: c=1/5, p_A=1/2, p_B=3/8 → c recovered exactly 1/5; (c=1/5,p_A=1,\n  p_B=3/8) vs (c=2/5,p_A=1,p_B=1/6) have identical moments → s_A = 0 → unidentified.\n- identity 1 − P_true − P_false = P_dissent holds in every case.\n\n## 10. Open, by name\nPartial value sharing (a factor firing without forcing one value) — a future model;\nprobe usefulness beyond the private-only case; discovery of sharing structure (never by a\nmeasure); adequacy policies (min cells, interval widths, budgets) as declared lines.\n\n## 11. Acceptance\nTypeScript implementation reproduces every captured vector exactly in rational\narithmetic; validation refuses by name; budget is computed before work; every result\ncarries model, handed parameters and conditions; states identical before and after;\nthe descriptive 0.4.0 measures and all algebra hashes unchanged.\n\n## 12. Capacity under a single dominant factor\n\n`capacityBound`, `capacityP0`, `mutualInformation`, and `unanimityNoFactor` use the declared single-dominant-factor model. T is uniform on n >= 2 values. F is independent of T with probability c. On F=1 all witnesses report one W, uniform among values different from T. On F=0 they use independent homogeneous private channels: correct with probability 1-p, otherwise uniform among n-1 wrong values. These hypotheses are handed, never inferred.\n\nFor every positive k, I(T;V) <= log2(n)-c*log2(n-1). The entropy chain rule gives\nH(T|V) = c*log2(n-1) + (1-c)*H(T|V,F=0) + I(T;F|V).\nThe first term is exclusion uncertainty, the second private uncertainty, the third factor ambiguity. A decoder cannot increase information about T.\n\nFor 0<p<1 and p!=(n-1)/n, the bound is attained as k tends to infinity: the empirical private vote distribution distinguishes T, and private unanimity has probability (1-p)^k+p^k/(n-1)^(k-1), tending to zero. For p=1,n>2 the missing value identifies T asymptotically. For p=1,n=2 every vote is the complement of T, giving one bit for every k. At p=0, I=log2(n)-h(c)-c*log2(n-1) for every k. At c=1, I=log2(n/(n-1)) for every k. At c=0 the uninformative private channel p=(n-1)/n gives zero for every k; c=0 does not by itself imply log2(n). At that uninformative p with a factor, the limit is c*log2(n/(n-1)), not the general upper bound.\n\nProbabilities and the conditional-entropy expression are exact rationals. Numerical entropy uses the explicitly handed `binary64-log2` policy, in bits, and is an approximation; it is not an exact rational logarithm. `mutualInformation` declares its joint-cell enumeration budget before work. `unanimityNoFactor` declares a rational-bit upper-bound budget before exponentiation. Budget exhaustion is unmeasured, never zero.\n\nCaptured finite information, n=4,c=1/5,p=3/10: k=1,2,4,7 gives approximately 0.313028978, 0.469712906, 0.914458010, 1.332321771 bits. The bound is approximately 1.683007500 bits. At p=0 the information is approximately 0.961079405 bits for every k. These decimals are illustrative approximations under the named policy.\n\n## 13. Decision results are separate\n\n`decisionInformation` accepts `plurality-uniform-ties@1` or `posterior-map-uniform-ties@1`. The second compares exact declared joint probabilities for finite k; it does not apply an intuition-based unanimity threshold. Ties are uniformly distributed among maximizers. It returns posterior information I(T;V), decision information I(T;answer), and exact rational decision accuracy as distinct quantities. The exact T/answer joint table accompanies them. A randomized answer cannot gain information over V.\n\nUnder p<(n-1)/n, asymptotically plurality emits the forced wrong value on the factor event. Posterior MAP identifies that event and samples uniformly among its n-1 alternatives. For n=4,c=1/5, plurality decision information is approximately 0.961079405 bits; MAP decision information is approximately 1.222162 bits; posterior information is approximately 1.683007500 bits. The two information gains differ. None is a finite-k or model-free guarantee.\n\n## 14. No-truth fitting\n\n`fitUnanimityModel` uses `exact-c-slices-on-declared-p-grid@1`. It takes one named lineage, cardinality, k/count/denominator rows, a declared p grid, exact absolute rate tolerance, and a work budget. For each handed p it intersects the exact inequalities for c, returning the entire compatible c interval and midpoint residual diagnostics. No claim is made about p values between slices or continuous identification. A measured fit means the declared procedure was evaluated; it does not mean parameters or causes were identified.\n\nThe observed rate is compared with c+(1-c)*[(1-p)^k+p^k/(n-1)^(k-1)]. Binary p versus 1-p ambiguity remains visible. Perfect unanimity leaves p=0 and c=1 indistinguishable. Too few informative sizes or no compatible slice are explicitly unmeasured. Mixed populations require a separately declared mixture model and are not pooled here.\n\nOptional bootstrap samples complete paired groups across sizes with a handed xorshift32 seed, B and random-word budget. It retains the full compatible regions in each replicate, not independent uncertainty intervals for each size. Groups are the declared resampling units; the procedure assumes exchangeable independent groups and does not establish that assumption. Counts alone leave uncertainty unmeasured. Replicate regions are empirical bootstrap output, not guaranteed confidence sets. Exhausted random words never produce successful uncertainty.\n\n## 15. False coincidence relative to a declared reference\n\n`coincidenceExcess` takes event/origin/value rows, truth rows (`kind=truth`), an explicit selection S and the `independent-uniform-wrong@1` reference. Order h is |S|; identity and redelivery never add origins or events. Values belong to the declared alphabet. Conflicting values or truth refuse. Incomplete shared events and insufficient declared event counts remain unmeasured. Imperfect probes need a separate contract and are not silently accepted as truth.\n\nThe reference uses handed error marginals, or explicitly estimated marginals stratified by observed truth. For handed homogeneous errors p_i, reference false coincidence is product(p_i)/(n-1)^(h-1). Estimated strata use their event weights, never a pooled error probability that confounds truth populations. Output contains the false-coincidence count, denominator, exact observed and reference rates, signed delta=observed-reference, and ratio=observed/reference. Delta is never floored at zero. At reference zero the ratio is unmeasured, while counts and delta remain available. No sampling design is inferred; uncertainty is separately unmeasured.\n\nA ratio greater than one means more coincidence than this reference, not a discovered common cause. Nonuniform wrong labels can exceed the uniform reference with independent witnesses. Independent errors with one shared wrong label give ratio (n-1)^(h-1): at n=64 this is 63 for pairs and 3969 for triples. The fair-bit XOR vector explicitly assigns one common wrong label: pairs have ratio 63, triples ratio zero and a negative delta. Indicators alone do not define the reported-value vector.\n\nUnder the separately handed dominant-factor model c=1/10,p=1/5,n=64, observed error marginals are 7/25. Relative to those marginals, delta at orders 2 and 3 is approximately 0.099326984 and 0.099996283. Its convergence to c is a conclusion under this model, never model-free identification.\n\n## 16. Research demands and boundaries\n\nThe spectrum summarizes covariance of binary error indicators. It is not a sufficient statistic of reported values, closure events or independence. False-unanimity excess summarizes one event and does not replace the full joint distribution. Higher-order closure statistics need a declared closure rule and the appropriate joint law of reported values given truth; neither is inferred from a form name.\n\nHelly is a property of a declared geometric family, not a probabilistic independence order or a truth guarantee. Geometry, factor precedence, partial label sharing, reproduction, percolation, time-varying fitting, imperfect-probe identification and model-free capacity remain explicitly open until their models, procedures and falsifiers close. Controlled experiments live in a world. No new origin, permission, algebraic amount or state is created by a measure.\n\n## 17. Publication and stability\n\nThe historical nine publication groups are linked to their contracts, yes/no data and declared Bound 0.1.7 captures in two places. The 0.4.2 operations below require new captures under their selected reader; historical implementation-bound receipts do not cover changed bytes. Validation readers are maintained TypeScript worlds with ordinary modular compilation; no .mjs bundle or private executor ships in this library. Their implementation inventory is retained once by digest. The installed archive must expose the same public exports, preserve the four states, match all published bytes and close native receipts without a private store. Declaring these requirements does not pay them.\n\n## 18. Declared failure bounds\n\n`unionBoundConfidence(failures)` requires distinct nonempty IDs, explicit rational probabilities in [0,1], and nonempty justification references. The caller must establish every failure bound in one probability space. References are retained, not verified. The returned lower bound for all events succeeding is max(0,1−Σp_i), with exact rational arithmetic. The empty family gives 1; Σp_i≥1 gives the trivial bound 0. No independence, truth probability, or algebraic state is inferred.\n\n`tolerantCountConfidence(failures,k)` requires a safe integer 0≤k≤m. For k<m it bounds failure by min(1,min_{0≤j≤k}(Σ of the m−j smallest p_i)/(k+1−j)); its exact success lower bound is the complement. For k=m success is certain. For m=3,k=1 and all p_i=1/10 the result is 17/20, not an inferred success rate. The distinct subset-intersection guarantee gives the weaker bound 7/10. Three disjoint paired failures among six events, each pair having probability 1/10, refute the former 4/5 claim: success is 7/10.\n\nThese operations compute conditional probability bounds. They select neither a geometric tolerance nor a consumer policy. Count-tolerant intersection T_k and widened-interval intersection T_eps remain named forms; tolerant-choice remains open.\n\n## 19. Declared covering cause\n\n`commonCauseCapacityBound(n,c,q,{logarithm:'binary64-log2'})` requires integer n≥2 and explicit rational c,q in [0,1]. Truth is uniform; the covering-cause indicator is independent of truth; when active, every report copies one symmetric channel with accuracy q. The inactive private channel is unrestricted. Revealing the indicator gives the upper bound log2(n)−c[h(q)+(1−q)log2(n−1)] in approximate bits. Copy count is no input. This is neither finite-sample mutual information nor an achievability claim.\n\nAt n=4,c=1,q=1/4 the bound is exactly zero; q=0 gives log2(4/3), and q=1 gives two bits. Missing q refuses. The operational declaration or detection of activation remains open in identification.model. Unconditioned coincidence does not identify q.\n\nM/3 and M/4 retain their existing definitions. Partial-factor governance proposals do not supply a conditional joint observation and selection law. The information carried by dissent, including a bottom symbol, is open for those unspecified proposals.\n\n## Obligations 0.4.3 — proposed conditional measures\n\nL7: conditional bound on one declared common probability space: P(N>k)<=min(1,sum over |S|=k+1 of min p_i), with rational declared marginal failure upper bounds and 0<=k<=m. Trivial iff computed upper bound is one. Counterexamples refute the former top-k+1 success guarantee. Joint-independent Bernoulli convolution is a separate declared hypothesis; rho=0 and pairwise independence do not supply it. Existing stronger 0.4.2 count bound is unchanged and needs no joint independence.\n\nL8: law by enumeration only for 3<=n<=64, positive integer divisors p|n and integer 0<=s<p. D=log2(n/p)-h(s/p), explicit binary64-log2 approximation. Odd-period minimum uses h(floor(p/2)/p), even-period minimum uses one. Complete maximum at zero; nonzero domain has endpoints 1,p-1, empty when p=1. No information or worst-case interpretation follows from nonnegativity. T87 retains its separate wrapped-phase amplitude identity.\n\nL9: law by counterexample of existence of noninjective weighted mix, and measure with a declared prior on a finite explicitly supplied set of named-factor configurations. Equal-weight ab and ba give the same bottom tie and different named forks. Loss is H(configuration | mix)=H(configuration)-H(mix); a uniform prior must be explicitly selected, absent prior is unmeasured. The four-state uniform example has mean loss 0.5 bits and one bit conditioned on tie; the injective named fork has zero loss. Defines no new algebraic act, governance selection, M/3 or M/4 law, or destroyed field.\n\n## Lineage and delivery: distinct scopes\n\nFor n>=2, uniform truth X, the declared n-ary symmetric channel X->Z with crossover probability c in [0,1], and X->Z->reports->aggregate A,\n\n```text\nH(X|Z) = h2(c) + c*log2(n-1) >= h2(c)\nH(X|A) >= H(X|Z)\nI(X;A) <= log2(n) - h2(c) - c*log2(n-1)\n```\n\nHere h2 is binary entropy, with 0*log2(0)=0. The uncertainty floor and information ceiling follow from this symmetric-channel equality and the declared Markov bottleneck. The reports and aggregation have no additional access to truth or a revealed error/cause indicator. This reuses capacityP0; it neither replaces capacityBound nor changes M/3 or M/4. An error rate alone, an asymmetric channel or the existing private-error model does not establish this ceiling. The distinguishing counterexamples and relation to F1 are in [the conditional lineage contract](../proofs/lineage-ceiling.json).\n\nThe parameter c denotes the mixture activation or channel crossover specified by the selected model. It is not identified from unanimity without that model, and no equality with the genetic inbreeding coefficient F is asserted. No genetic law follows here.\n\nCell vacuity is a property of a region under the algebra. Delivery asks whether the bytes named by a digest are available and match it. A vacuous cell neither certifies delivery nor proves that a delivered package is admissible. The relation between these predicates remains the named vacuity-versus-delivery tension.\n" at=witness:044/text-scope-corrections by=target form=alphabet measure=text role=writes scope=prose/en/measures shape=docs/measures.md value=lock
bound-lock/1 about="# Price\n\n<p align=\"center\"><img src=\"../img/price.svg\" alt=\"Two origins on one scale: seller claims 2 to 9 and buyer claims 5 to 12; what they hold together runs from a floor of 5 to a ceiling of 9.\" width=\"640\"></p>\n\nA claim from one origin is potential; a cell is information only where two independent origins touch it and their intervals meet.\n\n## Run it\n\n```ts\nimport { chain, disagree, freedom, meetAt, origins, point, unit } from '@lapxo/obligations';\n\nconst said = (cell: Parameters<typeof meetAt>[0]): string =>\n  (origins(cell) < 2 ? 'grey' : disagree(cell) ? 'split' : 'agreed');\n\nconst price = point('price', 0, [\n  { origin: 'seller', span: { lo: 2, hi: 9 } },\n  { origin: 'buyer', span: { lo: 5, hi: 12 } },\n]);\nconst scale = chain(Array.from({ length: 16 }, (_, i) => `l${i}`));\n\nconsole.log('verdict   ', said(price));\nconsole.log('they hold ', JSON.stringify(meetAt(price)));\nconsole.log('still free', freedom(scale, unit('price', { floor: 'l5', ceiling: 'l9' })).toFixed(2), 'bits');\nconsole.log('why       a seller and a buyer are two origins; where their marks meet is a price, and what is left between the marks is what nobody has decided yet');\n```\n\n```\nverdict    agreed\nthey hold  {\"lo\":5,\"hi\":9}\nstill free 2.32 bits\nwhy       a seller and a buyer are two origins; where their marks meet is a price, and what is left between the marks is what nobody has decided yet\n```\n\nIts origins meet.\n\n## What it rests on\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n  c0[\"point\"]\n  c1[\"unit\"]\n  l0([\"encounter\"])\n  l1([\"debit in bits\"])\n  c0 --> l0\n  c1 --> l1\n  classDef shown fill:#2da44e,color:#ffffff,stroke:#2da44e\n  class l0 shown\n```\n\n## Source\n\n[Its source](../../examples/release/price.ts) is one of the examples of obligations, and it runs as it is written, against the built package, with nothing compiled for it.\n" at=witness:041/retain-declared-documentation by=target form=alphabet measure=text role=writes scope=prose/en/learn-price shape=docs/learn/price.md value=lock
bound-lock/1 about="# Reference\n\nEvery operation this package offers, every law it states and every form it declares: 242 operations, 126 laws, 4 forms.\n\n## Notation\n\n```text\nc = ⟨⊥, ⊤, O, ⊑⟩        over L at r\n```\n\n| formula | law | sentence |\n|---|---|---|\n| `meet(c) = ⊤ ∧ ⋀_{o∈O} span_o` | meet | two bounds on one cell fold by the meet of their form; the order they arrive in never decides |\n| `info(c) ⟺ \\|ext(O)\\| ≥ 2 ∧ meet(c) ≠ ∅` | encounter | a claim from one origin is potential; a cell is information only where two independent origins touch it and their intervals meet |\n| `state(c) ∈ {FREE, REQUIRED, FORBIDDEN, CONFLICT}` | four states | one origin is required; two or more that meet within the ceiling are free; that meet outside it are forbidden; that do not meet are in conflict; fewer states collapse two of them |\n| `x ⊨ c ⟺ ⊥ ⊑ x ⊑ ⊤` | carrier | every quantity has two poles, a floor that is required and a ceiling that is permitted, and a cell is the pair |\n| `F(c) = log₂ \\|{x ∈ L : ⊥ ≤ x ≤ ⊤}\\|` | debit in bits | the debit of a cell is log2 of the states between its floor and ceiling; a closed cell has zero |\n| `claim = (o, span, e), e ≺ e′ ⟺ e → e′` | epoch | every claim carries when it holds; order is causal, never a clock |\n| `ext(o) = {(x, span_o(x))}, ext(o) = ext(o′) ⟹ o = o′` | origin is extension | an origin is the set of (coordinate, interval) it has produced; two origins with one extension are one; independence is distance between extensions |\n| `o ⊢_{r,e} c for o, c ∈ C, and ⊢ ∈ C` | everything is a bound | one relation: an origin bounds a cell at a resolution and an epoch; cells, origins, locks and laws are cells under it; the relation bounds itself, and there is no level above it |\n| `sign: ⊤(c′) ← ⊤(c′) ∧ s ∀ c′ ⊒ c` | signatures are meets | a signature narrows a ceiling and travels to every cell that rests on it as a meet, and meets do not remember the order they were taken in, so no order of any number of signatures ever shows in the field they leave; widening is not a meet and does not travel, which is why a narrowing costs one signature and an error costs one at every cell, and folding the field and signing it commute even with a withdrawal present |\n| `F(C) = Σ_{c∈C} F(c), F(a(c)) ≤ F(c) for every act a that narrows` | freedom conserved | the freedom of a system is the sum of its cells' debits in bits; an encounter never raises it; only a new coordinate does |\n| `⊥(c) = ⋁ required ∨ ⋁_{o∈O} lo_o` | the obligatory | the object is c = ⟨⊥, ⊤, O, ⊑⟩ over L at r: four parts in two pairs, the value (⊥, ⊤) and the relations (O, ⊑), with the lattice L it is given and the resolution r it is read at belonging to the reading and the sign to the act; an origin is (o, φ), and the poles are read, never stored: ⊥ is what is required joined with what its live claims hold, ⊤ what is signed met with what they allow; eight acts in four pairs and seven names answer for it: meet and join on the ceiling, local, a join only with a witness; observe and withdraw on the floor, local, a withdrawal being an observation at −1 that restores exactly; sign and require on both poles, travelling by rest, commuting and both closing freedom; encounter and refine with no pole; and no widening travels |\n| `⊤(c) = ⋀ signed ∧ ⋀_{o∈O} hi_o` | the obligatory | the object is c = ⟨⊥, ⊤, O, ⊑⟩ over L at r: four parts in two pairs, the value (⊥, ⊤) and the relations (O, ⊑), with the lattice L it is given and the resolution r it is read at belonging to the reading and the sign to the act; an origin is (o, φ), and the poles are read, never stored: ⊥ is what is required joined with what its live claims hold, ⊤ what is signed met with what they allow; eight acts in four pairs and seven names answer for it: meet and join on the ceiling, local, a join only with a witness; observe and withdraw on the floor, local, a withdrawal being an observation at −1 that restores exactly; sign and require on both poles, travelling by rest, commuting and both closing freedom; encounter and refine with no pole; and no widening travels |\n| `A(c) = \\|Σ_{o∈O} s_o w_o e^{iφ_o}\\|²` | amplitude has sign and phase | a claim carries a size and a direction, how much it narrows and the turn it landed on, and a withdrawal is the same size the other way: two hundred claims in phase make the square of their number and the same two hundred strewn make its order, while a write and its withdrawal at one epoch leave nothing and at different epochs do not cancel |\n| `⊤(c) = ⋀_{b ⊑ c} signed(b) ∧ ⋀_{o∈O} hi_o, one line per act` | signatures live where they are signed | a signature or requirement is a line at the bound, never a value copied into resting cells; a cell's poles are read up its chain of rest as the meet of the live signatures above it; an absorbed signature stays live so withdrawing a tighter one reveals it. Signing and withdrawing cost one line; reading costs the depth of rest; nothing propagates, and A2 with T3 makes the reading equal to any replay. |\n| `\\|e^{ia} + e^{ib}\\|² < 2 ⟺ \\|a − b\\| > π/2` | the quarter turn | two claims reinforce within a quarter turn of each other and cancel beyond it, since \\|e^{ia} + e^{ib}\\|² < 2 exactly when \\|a − b\\| > π/2: a fork is destructive interference, and a field is laminar while fewer than half its pairs lie beyond a quarter turn, forks over closes below one |\n| `state(c) ∈ {FREE, REQUIRED, FORBIDDEN, CONFLICT}` | state | a cell is in one of four states: FREE, REQUIRED, FORBIDDEN, CONFLICT — green, grey, red, fork are their renders |\n| `frag(c) = min { \\|W\\| : W ⊆ O(c), ⊥(c∖W) ≠ ⊥(c) or ⊤(c∖W) ≠ ⊤(c) }` | fragility is the fewest withdrawals | a cell is as fragile as the fewest origins whose withdrawal moves one of its poles: a pole one origin sets breaks with that origin, and a pole several origins set together holds until all of them withdraw |\n\n```text\ncell  : at ×  × [Claim] × [restsOn] → Obligatory\nparts : Lattice × Obligatory × World → Cell\nstate : Lattice × Obligatory × World → state\nmark  : Mark → Mark\nlive  : [L] → [L]\nstate(c) ∈ {FREE, REQUIRED, FORBIDDEN, CONFLICT}\n```\n\n## States\n\n```mermaid\nstateDiagram-v2\n  state \"FREE · green\" as FREE\n  state \"REQUIRED · grey\" as REQUIRED\n  state \"FORBIDDEN · red\" as FORBIDDEN\n  state \"CONFLICT · fork\" as CONFLICT\n  FORBIDDEN --> CONFLICT: dangerous\n  FORBIDDEN --> FREE: a witnessed join reopened it\n  FREE --> CONFLICT: dangerous\n  FREE --> FORBIDDEN: a signature closed below what they hold\n  FREE --> REQUIRED: a withdrawal took one back\n  REQUIRED --> CONFLICT: dangerous\n  REQUIRED --> FREE: a second origin met the first\n  classDef green fill:#2da44e,color:#ffffff,stroke:#2da44e\n  classDef grey fill:#8c959f,color:#ffffff,stroke:#8c959f\n  classDef red fill:#cf222e,color:#ffffff,stroke:#cf222e\n  classDef fork fill:#8250df,color:#ffffff,stroke:#8250df\n  class FREE green\n  class REQUIRED grey\n  class FORBIDDEN red\n  class CONFLICT fork\n```\n\n## What the object rests on\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart BT\n  o([\"the obligatory\"])\n  r0([\"carrier\"])\n  r1([\"encounter\"])\n  r2([\"meet\"])\n  r3([\"epoch\"])\n  r4([\"origin is extension\"])\n  r5([\"freedom conserved\"])\n  r6([\"everything is a bound\"])\n  o --> r0\n  o --> r1\n  o --> r2\n  o --> r3\n  o --> r4\n  o --> r5\n  o --> r6\n  classDef object fill:#2da44e,color:#ffffff,stroke:#2da44e\n  class o object\n```\n\n## Operations\n\n| operation | view | signature | refuses | law |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| anyOf | chain | `anyOf(c: Scale, units: readonly Unit[]): Unit[]` | 1 | no law |\n| coarsen | chain | `coarsen(place: Point, steps: number): Point` | 1 | no law |\n| composeState | chain | `composeState(c: Scale, units: readonly Unit[]): State` | 1 | no law |\n| conflictDebits | chain | `conflictDebits(c: Scale, u: Unit): number` | 1 | no law |\n| downset | chain | `downset(c: Scale, names: readonly string[]): readonly string[]` | 2 | D2-ideal |\n| entails | chain | `entails(c: Scale, a: Unit, b: Unit): boolean` | 1 | no law |\n| foldRegion | chain | `foldRegion<T>(places: readonly Point[], where: Region, f: (met: readonly Point[]) => T): readonly Fold<T>[]` | 1 | no law |\n| freedom | chain | `freedom(c: Scale, u: Unit): number` | 3 | no law |\n| missing | chain | `missing(c: Scale, have: Unit, want: Unit): { raiseFloorTo: string[]; raiseCeilingTo: string[] }` | 2 | no law |\n| size | chain | `size(at: readonly string[], steps?: number): number` | 1 | A3-resolution |\n| sufficientResolution | chain | `sufficientResolution<T>(finest: number, verdict: (steps: number) => T, same: (a: T, b: T) => boolean = Object.is): number` | 1 | T7-sufficient-resolution |\n| unit | chain | `unit(subject: string, bounds: { floor: string; ceiling: string }): Unit` | 1 | D4-debit-in-bits |\n| unitToIdeal | chain | `unitToIdeal(c: Scale, u: Unit): IdealDebit` | 2 | no law |\n| upset | chain | `upset(c: Scale, names: readonly string[]): readonly string[]` | 2 | D2-ideal |\n| weakest | chain | `weakest(methods: Iterable<Method>): Method` | 2 | D4-debit-in-bits |\n| and | complement | `and(a: Packed, b: Packed): Packed` | 13 | no law |\n| asBoolean | complement | `asBoolean(c: Scale, u: Unit): BoolView` | 1 | no law |\n| bitLoss | complement | `bitLoss(c: Scale): { readonly possible: number; readonly exactly: number; readonly lossy: number; readonly unsayable: number; }` | 1 | no law |\n| canonical | complement | `canonical(pk: Packer, p: Packed): Packed` | 2 | T8-exact-withdrawal |\n| opinion | complement | `opinion(demanded: number, permitted: number, limiting: number, required: number): Packed` | 2 | T8-exact-withdrawal |\n| amplitude | field | `amplitude(claim: Claim): number` | 2 | T49-amplitude-has-sign-and-phase |\n| bites | field | `bites<T>(L: Lattice<T>, world: World<T>, resting: ReadonlyMap<string, readonly string[]>, name: string, span: T): { readonly cells: readonly string[]; readonly visits: number }` | 2 | T94-a-signature-stops-where-the-