bound-lock/1 about="The extremal — For every $n\\ge1$, \\[ c_n\\;=\\;\\log_2 n-2+\\frac{2}{n}, \\] and for $n\\ge3$ the maximum is attained exactly at the following configuration and its relabellings (for $n\\le2$ the maximum is $0$ and every pair attains it): identify the $n$ states with the $n$ edges of a path on $n+1$ vertices; let $x$ group the edges sharing an even-numbered endpoint and $y$ those sharing an odd-numbered endpoint. These have $\\lceil (n+1)/2\\rceil$ and $\\lfloor (n+1)/2\\rfloor$ blocks, every block of size $1$ or $2$, trivial meet and discrete join." at=policy:law/thm-extremal by=target form=alphabet kind=theorem measure=status needs=samples/yes/thm-extremal.json|test/f1-boundary-042.test.ts|vectors/laws/f1/path-witness.json restsOn=law/lem-identity|law/lem-join|law/lem-meet|law/lem-phi role=demands scope=law/thm-extremal value=present
bound-lock/1 about="The graph identity — If $x\\wedge y=\\bot$ then \\[ \\RI_H(x,y)\\;=\\;\\log_2 n-\\frac{\\Phi(x,y)}{n}, \\qquad \\Phi(x,y)=\\sum_{e}c_e\\log_2\\frac{a_e\\,b_e}{c_e}, \\] the sum running over the edges of $G(x,y)$, where $c_e$ is the weight of $e$ and $a_e,b_e$ are the sizes of the two blocks it joins. Domain: two partitions of a nonempty finite uniformly weighted universe, with trivial common meet. Proof: H(X)=log2(n)-sum_a a*log2(a)/n, likewise for Y, and H(X,Y)=log2(n)-sum_c c*log2(c)/n. Each row and column of the intersection table sums to its block size, so sum_c c*log2(a*b/c)=sum_a a*log2(a)+sum_b b*log2(b)-sum_c c*log2(c). Substitute into H(X)+H(Y)-H(X,Y)." at=policy:law/lem-identity by=target form=alphabet kind=theorem measure=status needs=samples/yes/lem-identity.json|test/laws-papers.test.ts|test/f1-boundary-042.test.ts|vectors/laws/f1/path-witness.json restsOn=law/A0-carrier|law/A2-meet|law/D5-declaration role=demands scope=law/lem-identity value=present
