bound-lock/1 about="floor_limit_diag: vector plus written proof, NOT a general Lean theorem. For 0<=c<1, p in (1/2,1), cumulative-floor quotas and S(A)=1/2, variance is order N and quota mean shift is O(1); the standard independent-array normal approximation yields error tending to 1/2. Finite vector: K=1,p=3/4,c=1/3,w=[1], N=1..12; A=0 has q=1/2, A=1 has q=5/6, target floor=1/8. The finite unbiased diagonal identity is kernel-checked; it does not establish a general limit. theta=0 belongs to this diagonal and retains mass (1-p)^K/2. c=1 is excluded: its zero conditional variance needs a separate tie-preserving allocation contract.\n" at=witness:obligations045 by=target form=alphabet measure=text role=writes scope=prose/en/contracts/m0-limit-diag shape=docs/contracts/m0-limit-diag.md value=lock
bound-lock/1 about="floor_limit_offdiag_M0 and floor_limit_offdiag_mixture: conditional on each fixed latent A, actual jointly independent Bernoulli lineage draws with mean q_l=(1-c)p+c A_l satisfy lineage_lgn. The proved cumulative-floor allocation gives member_average_lgn. If S(A)=sum w_l q_l differs from 1/2, the fair-majority loss tends almost surely to 1{S<1/2}; dominated convergence gives its probability limit. The mixture theorem requires no S(A)=1/2 for every latent state. Technical hypotheses: integrability, identical distribution, independence and measurability are explicit in the formal statement. No ConditionalTailLimits or CLT is assumed by the off-diagonal theorem. The Lean probability formulation uses actual lineage arrays and integrals; equivalence to the separate computational binomial-sum definition is not kernel-proved here.\n" at=witness:obligations045 by=target form=alphabet measure=text role=writes scope=prose/en/contracts/m0-limit-offdiag shape=docs/contracts/m0-limit-offdiag.md value=lock
bound-lock/1 about="floor_quota_bounds: K>=1, named nonnegative weights summing to one, p in (1/2,1), c in [0,1). Set W_i=sum_{j<i}w_j, n_i(N)=floor(N W_{i+1})-floor(N W_i). Lean proves sum n_i=N, -1<n_i-Nw_i<1, n_i/N tends to w_i, positive-share counts tend to infinity and zero-share counts vanish. Transport counts, membership and weights under permutation.\n" at=witness:obligations045 by=target form=alphabet measure=text role=writes scope=prose/en/contracts/m0-allocation shape=docs/contracts/m0-allocation.md value=lock
